{"name":"Ares Shadow","hex":"#62584c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ares","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of are"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ares"},{"term":"shadow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dark image projected onto a surface where light (or other radiation) is blocked by the shade of an object.","Relative darkness, especially as caused by the interruption of light; gloom; obscurity."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unofficial, informal, unauthorized, but acting as though it were.","Having power or influence, but not widely known or recognized."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shadow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ares","blocks":[{"text":"Ares is the Greek god of war and courage. He is one of the Twelve Olympians, and the son of Zeus and Hera. Many Greeks were ambivalent towards him. He embodies the physical valor necessary for success in war but can also personify sheer brutality and bloodlust, in contrast to his sister Athena, whose martial functions include military strategy and generalship. An association with Ares endows places, objects, and other deities with a savage, dangerous, or militarized quality."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/%CE%9F_%CE%86%CF%81%CE%B7%CF%82_%28Borghese-%CE%9B%CE%BF%CF%8D%CE%B2%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%85%29.jpg/330px-%CE%9F_%CE%86%CF%81%CE%B7%CF%82_%28Borghese-%CE%9B%CE%BF%CF%8D%CE%B2%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%85%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ares"},{"title":"Shadow","blocks":[{"text":"A shadow is a dark area on a surface where light from a light source is blocked by an object. In contrast, shade occupies the three-dimensional volume behind an object with light in front of it. The cross-section of a shadow is a two-dimensional silhouette, or a reverse projection of the object blocking the light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg/330px-The_shadow_of_a_musician_playing_transverse_flute.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg/500px-Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg/1280px-Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg/500px-Ares_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ares, 2nd–3rd century AD, after a Greek bronze original by Alkamenes dated 420 BC,[citation needed] excavated in 1925 in Rome's Largo di Torre Argentina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAres_Argentina_Montemartini.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG/500px-Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG/1280px-Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG/500px-Parthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ares (right) with Demeter, Dionysus and Hermes on the frieze of the Parthenon, c. 447–433 BC, British Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParthenon_frieze_east_IV_fragment.JPG"},{"srcset